O|                                                                      1.            
O|
S,M|       301  INT. DUCTS - WHERE WE WERE AT THE END OF EPISODE 2            301       
O|
N|            PANICKED BREATHING. DARKNESS. WATER RUSHING.                            
O|
N|            Then: whir whir whir whir...                                            
N|            A SMALL, DIM LIGHT begins to fade up in the darkness,                   
N|            PULSING brighter and brighter with each whir whir whir...               
O|
N|            BEZPALOV - is holding a small hand-powered DYNO TORCH. With             
N|            every squeeze, the light grows brighter... then starts to               
N|            dim down...                                                             
O|
N|            BARANOV moves the DOSIMETER closer to the meager rising/                
N|            falling light. Then signals to Ananenko... look at this...              
N|            but Ananenko shakes his head. Doesn't matter. Stay calm.                
O|
N|            whir whir whirwhirwhirwhirwhir                                          
N|            Ananenko makes gentle movements now with his hand. To both              
N|            of them. "Slow your breathing. Don't panic."                            
O|
N|            whir... whir....    whir.......                                         
O|
N|            The three men wrestle with their fear. Control their                    
N|            breathing. Then Ananenko grabs Bezpalov by the wrist. Moves             
N|            Bezpalov's free hand toward the back of his wetsuit.                    
O|
N|            Grab it.                                                                
O|
N|            Bezpalov grabs it. Then Baranov grabs on to him, so that all            
N|            three are now holding on to each other.                                 
N|            Ananenko holds his hand up to them. Makes a FIST. "Tight."              
O|
N|            They tighten their grips. If they let go... they are lost.              
O|
N|            whir whir whir whir...                                                  
O|
N|            Bezpalov moves the dyno torch around so they can see:                   
O|
N|            THE WALL OF THE DUCT.                                                   
O|
N|            Water continues to slowly fill the space                                
N|            Ananenko moves slowly toward the wall... the two other men              
N|            following along in a chain...                                           
O|
N|            When they get to the wall, Ananenko grabs hold of a PIPE.               
N|            There are SIX pipes running along the wall. He counts                   
N|            down... first, second, third, fourth.                                   
O|
O|
O|                                                                      2.            
O|
N|            Fourth. That's the one that leads to the sluice gate. He                
N|            begins FEELING HIS WAY forward, hand over hand, never losing            
N|            contact with the fourth pipe...                                         
O|
N|            ...and the others follow in the near darkness.                          
N|            whir whir whir...                                                       
O|
S|       302  EXT. CHERNOBYL - VICINITY OF THE HATCH - SAME                 302       
O|
N|            Pikalov, Shcherbina and Legasov stand waiting. No one says a            
N|            word. Then Shcherbina checks his watch.                                 
O|
N|            Legasov says nothing. Just keeps staring at the hatch.                  
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Is it possible the water has already                       
U|                         killed them?                                               
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Yes.                                                       
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         So then what?                                              
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         If it doesn't work?                                        
O|
N|            Legasov doesn't answer. Because there is no answer.                     
O|
S|       303  INT. DUCTS - SAME                                             303       
O|
N|            Dark. It's deeper here. And emerging from the pitch black...            
O|
N|            ...the three men. Holding tight to each other. Following                
N|            that single pipe. Their only hope. The barest of light                  
N|            coming from Bezpalov, whose hand is getting tired.                      
O|
N|            The pulsing of the torch is lower... lower...                           
O|
N,G|            They get closer to us. Closer. Closer. Breathing louder.                
N|            Water rising up to their WAIST. And then:                               
N|            A JARRING NOISE                                                         
O|
N,G|            REVERSE TO SEE: in the darkness, Ananenko has walked right              
N|            into the CLOSED SLUICE GATE. His MASK is knocked askew.                 
O|
N|            BARANOV - moves quickly. Adjusts the mask back onto                     
N|            Baranov's face.                                                         
O|
N|            whirwhirwhirwhirwhir                                                    
O|
O|
O|                                                                      3.            
O|
N|            Breathe. You're okay. Look. We did it. We found the gate.               
O|
N|            On the sluice gate - TWO RUSTY WHEEL VALVES on either side.             
N|            Ananenko wades to one. Bezpalov gives Baranov the dyno                  
N|            torch, and moves to the other.                                          
N|            The valves look as if they have never been turned.                      
O|
N|            If they can't open them, then this was for nothing. The men             
N|            put their hands on the valves.                                          
O|
N|            Look at each other. And then: THEY PUT their all into it...             
N,G|            and before we can even see if the wheels budge--                        
O|
S|       304  EXT. CHERNOBYL - VICINITY OF THE HATCH - LATER                304       
O|
N|            Shcherbina paces. Pikalov stares at the ground.                         
N|            Legasov stares silently at the hatch.                                   
O|
N|            And then: a noise... loud enough that it can be heard even              
N|            from here. A heavy THUMP THUMP THUMP on the hatch.                      
O|
N|            Soldiers rush over. Open the hatch.                                     
O|
N|            BEZPALOV emerges. Soaked from the waist down, and SHOUTING              
N|            in triumph.                                                             
O|
N|            The soldiers BURST into cheers and applause as Baranov and              
N|            Ananenko follow Bezpalov out...                                         
N|            Legasov, Shcherbina and Pikalov all slump with relief. They             
N|            did it. Shcherbina motions to Pikalov, who immediately heads            
N|            toward some military firemen.                                           
O|
C|                                     PIKALOV                                        
U|                         Bring the hoses in. Start pumping.                         
O|
N|            Shcherbina CLAPS Legasov on the shoulder. Beaming.                      
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Look. Look at our boys!                                    
N,G|            ON THE THREE - surrounded by cheering soldiers. They look               
N|            absolutely fine. Bezpalov takes a swig of vodka.                        
O|
N|            It's a jubilant scene, but Legasov looks as if he's going to            
N|            throw up.                                                               
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Get them to the doctors.                                   
O|
O|
O|                                                                      4.            
O|
N|            Legasov walks away. Shcherbina absorbs that, then looks back            
N|            at the scene. The men continue to hug and congratulate the              
N|            divers and each other, even as the military firefighters                
N|            bring in the pump hoses...                                              
N|            Ananenko catches sight of Shcherbina. The man who told him              
N|            it must be done.                                                        
O|
N|            The smile fades from Ananenko's face. Replaced with the                 
N|            simple pride of a man who knows he may have just given his              
N|            life to his people.                                                     
O|
N|            He raises a bottle to Shcherbina, and Shcherbina solemnly               
N|            nods back.                                                              
O|
S|       305  EXT. NOVAYA BASMANAYA STREET - MOSCOW - AFTERNOON             305       
N|            A large, imposing stone building set back from the street.              
N|            Two stories, with tall windows in orderly rows.                         
O|
N,I,G|            LYUDMILLA, the firefighter's wife, enters frame, carrying               
N|            her dingy suitcase. A country mouse far from home.                      
O|
N|            She stops in front of the building. Looks up at it. She's               
N|            never felt so small or afraid in her life.                              
O|
M|            TITLE:                                                                  
O|
M|                                   APRIL 30, 1986                                   
M|                              HOSPITAL NUMBER 6, MOSCOW                             
O|
S|       306  INT. HOSPITAL NUMBER 6 - MOMENTS LATER                        306       
O|
N|            Lyudmilla approaches the receptionist at the front desk.                
O|
N,I|            The receptionist, YENINA, 30, is filling out some paperwork.            
N|            Lyudmilla just stands there, waiting silently.                          
O|
N|            Finally, Yenina STAMPS the paperwork, removes her reading               
N|            glasses, and:                                                           
O|
C|                                     YENINA                                         
U|                         Yes?                                                       
N|            Lyudmilla dutifully recites what the military officer told              
N|            her to say.                                                             
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         I'm here to see my husband, Vasily                         
U|                         Ignatenko. He's a firefighter from                         
U|                         Chernobyl. I have permission.                              
O|
O|
O|                                                                      5.            
O|
C|                                     YENINA                                         
U|                         Chernobyl? I'm sorry. No visitors.                         
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         But-- Major Burov, he told me-- he                         
U|                         said--                                                     
C|                                     YENINA                                         
U|                         No exceptions.                                             
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         Please-- I came all the way from                           
U|                         Kievskaya oblast...                                        
O|
N|            Yenina is unmoved. Just keeps staring.                                  
O|
N|            Lyudmilla puts her suitcase down, and opens her purse. Digs             
N|            out some crumpled MONEY. Holds it out silently to the                   
N|            receptionist.                                                           
N|            Yenina looks at the money, then grabs a small slip of paper,            
N|            selects a STAMP from her wheel, and-- TWHACK. Stamps a                  
N|            visitor's pass.                                                         
O|
N|            She takes Lyudmilla's money, and hands her the pass.                    
O|
S|       307  INT. HALLWAY - RADIATION WING - MOMENTS LATER                 307       
O|
N|            Lyudmilla walks down the hall with her suitcase and her                 
N|            stamped pass.                                                           
N,I|            She sees a DOCTOR, VETROVA, 30's, wearing a cloth face mask.            
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         Excuse me?                                                 
O|
N|            Vetrova turns. Surprised to see a visitor. She lowers her               
N|            face mask.                                                              
O|
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
U|                         Who are you? What are you doing up                         
U|                         here?                                                      
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         I have a pass.                                             
O|
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
U|                         You can't be here. It's not safe.                          
O|
N|            Lyudmilla starts into her recitation.                                   
O|
O|
O|                                                                      6.            
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         I'm here to see my husband, Vasily                         
U|                         Ignatenko. He's a firefighter from--                       
O|
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
U|                         I know who Ignatenko is, but you                           
U|                         can't.                                                     
N|            Lyudmilla tries to hold back her tears. It's not working.               
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         But I have permission. I have--                            
O|
N|            Lyudmilla opens her purse once more. There's not much left.             
O|
N|            Vetrova realizes what Lyudmilla's doing. Oh god. She puts               
N|            her hand on Lyudmilla's. No need for that...                            
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
P|                               (reluctant)                                          
U|                         You can have thirty minutes with him.                      
U|                         Not a minute more.                                         
O|
N|            Lyudmilla, relieved, nods.                                              
O|
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
U|                         And you cannot touch him. In any way.                      
U|                         Do you understand?                                         
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         Yes.                                                       
N|            The doctor hesitates, then:                                             
O|
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
U|                         Room 15.                                                   
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
P|                               (overwhelmed)                                        
U|                         Thank you.                                                 
O|
N|            As Lyudmilla starts to walk down the hall--                             
O|
C|                                     VETROVA                                        
U|                         You're not pregnant, are you?                              
N|            Lyudmilla stops. Turns back to the doctor.                              
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         No.                                                        
O|
N|            The doctor nods. Go on then.                                            
O|
O|
O|                                                                      7.            
O|
S|       308  INT. HOSPITAL HALLWAY / ROOM 15 - MOMENTS LATER               308       
O|
N|            Lyudmilla rounds a corner. Room 12... 13... 14... Room 15.              
N|            She screws up her courage, and then pushes the door open.               
N|            REVEAL: the FIREFIGHTERS... Vasily sitting up on one bed,               
N|            Kibenok and Pravik sitting side-by-side on the other.                   
O|
N|            Pravik is hooked up to an IV. And they're each in hospital              
N|            pyjamas. Skin reddened, as if sunburned. But otherwise?                 
O|
N|            They're fine. Playing cards, in fact. Pravik looks up.                  
O|
C|                                     PRAVIK                                         
U|                         Look who the cat dragged in.                               
O|
N|            Vasily sees Lyudmilla. A big grin breaks out on his face.               
C|                                     VASILY                                         
U|                         What did I tell you? There's no                            
U|                         hiding from this woman...                                  
O|
N|            The firefighters laugh. Vasily rises as Lyudmilla drops her             
N|            suitcase and RUSHES into his arms, hugging and kissing him              
N|            as she weeps for joy.                                                   
O|
C|                                     VASILY                                         
U|                         Ow ow... easy! Easy!                                       
O|
N|            She backs off slightly, but just keeps sobbing on him.                  
C|                                     VASILY                                         
U|                         It's okay. Shhh, Lyusya, shhh. I'm                         
U|                         okay. We're all okay...                                    
O|
N|            SOUND RISES: military drums, a massive crowd cheering, and:             
O|
S,M|       309  EXT. RED SQUARE - MOSCOW - DAY [FILE FOOTAGE]                 309       
O|
N,M|            FILE FOOTAGE - A bright, sunny days. Thousands of people.               
N|            Red flags everywhere. Children in uniforms. Soldiers                    
N|            marching in formation. Trucks display nuclear missiles.                 
M|            TITLE:                                                                  
O|
M|                                     MAY 1, 1986                                    
M|                              INTERNATIONAL LABOUR DAY                              
O|
M|                             5 DAYS AFTER THE EXPLOSION                             
O|
O|
O|                                                                      8.            
O|
S|       310  INT. GARANIN'S OFFICE - MINSK - SAME                          310       
O|
N|            Garanin is on the phone. Through the window, we hear the                
N|            cheering of PEOPLE on the street.                                       
C|                                     GARANIN                                        
U|                         Yes, the parade's already begun. All                       
U|                         of Minsk is out on the street, which                       
U|                         is why I felt it was--                                     
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         Of course not-- but the wind has been                      
U|                         blowing in from the southeast since                        
U|                         early morning. From Kiev. From                             
U|                         Cherno-- no, no... but if we were to                       
U|                         cancel now, just as a precaution-- I                       
U|                         understand, but then perhaps we                            
U|                         should issue iodine tablets to--                           
N|            He's been cut off again. Then, defeated:                                
O|
C|                                     GARANIN                                        
U|                         Yes. Certainly you're right. It was                        
U|                         foolish of me to call. I apologize.                        
U|                         Thank you for your wisdom, Minister.                       
U|                         Please-- enjoy the 100th anniversary                       
U|                         of International Workers' Day.                             
O|
N|            Garanin places the phone receiver back on the cradle. Then              
N,G|            walks to the window. We can't see the parade. We can only               
N,G|            see him watching helplessly.                                            
N|            He picks up his jacket, which is covered in civilian                    
N|            MEDALS-- the ceremonial finery of a Party leader. He puts it            
N|            on, buttons it, straightens it, takes a breath--                        
O|
N|            --and heads out to join the parade.                                     
O|
D|       311  OMITTED                                                       311       
O|
S|       312  INT. MILITARY HELICOPTER - DAY                                312       
O|
N|            Mid-flight. A RADIOMETRIC CREW is in the back. Full                     
N|            protective suits. The door is open, and they're holding                 
N|            RADIOMETERS out to sample the smoky air.                                
N|            We hear the telltale CRACKLE of a GEIGER COUNTER... and as              
N|            the helicopter washes through a PLUME OF SMOKE, the clicking            
N|            suddenly explodes into DEAFENING STATIC...                              
O|
O|
O|                                                                      9.            
O|
S|       313  EXT. REACTOR SITE - SAME                                      313       
O|
N|            The helicopter is CIRCLING above the destroyed reactor.                 
O|
M|            TITLE:                                                                  
M|                                     MAY 2, 1986                                    
O|
S|       314  EXT. REACTOR SITE - LATER                                     314       
O|
N|            Trailers have been set up near the site as mobile offices. A            
N|            web of FIRE HOSES extend out from the ruins toward Pikalov's            
N|            specialized military fire trucks, each with pumps running.              
O|
N|            FLOOD LIGHTS have been set up to illuminate the work area.              
N|            Men move back and forth, some wearing gas masks. Others not.            
N|            PIKALOV - talks with one of the radiometrists who was on the            
N|            helicopter. Then walks back to:                                         
O|
N|            LEGASOV and SHCHERBINA - who stand over a workbench covered             
N|            in maps. They're mid-argument.                                          
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         How did this happen? Who gave them                         
U|                         this idea?                                                 
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Are you suggesting I did?                                  
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Well someone decided the evacuation                        
U|                         zone should be thirty kilometers,                          
U|                         when we know--                                             
P|                               (points to the map)                                  
U|                         Here! Caesium-137 in Gomel District.                       
U|                         Two HUNDRED kilometers away!                               
O|
N|            Pikalov waits. Uncomfortable. Clearly has information to                
N|            share, but doesn't want to interrupt.                                   
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         It was decided.                                            
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Based on WHAT?                                             
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         I don't know.                                              
O|
O|
O|                                                                     10.            
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
P|                               (disbelief)                                          
U|                         Forgive me. Maybe I've spent too much                      
U|                         time in my lab. Or maybe I'm stupid.                       
U|                         But is this really how it all works?                       
U|                         An uninformed, arbitrary decision                          
U|                         that will cost who knows how many                          
U|                         lives is made by some apparatchik?                         
U|                         Some career Party man?                                     
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
P|                               (angered)                                            
U|                         I am a career Party man. You should                        
U|                         mind your tone, Comrade Legasov.                           
O|
N|            Legasov stares oddly at Shcherbina. Wasn't expecting that               
N|            defense. It almost seemed... calculated.                                
C|                                     PIKALOV                                        
P|                               (ahem)                                               
U|                         Comrades.                                                  
O|
N|            They turn to him. He's holding a piece of paper in his hand.            
O|
C|                                     PIKALOV                                        
U|                         We have visual confirmation that the                       
U|                         fire is nearly extinguished. There                         
U|                         has also been a reduction in iodine-                       
U|                         131 and caesium-137 emissions.                             
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Good.                                                      
P|                               (to Legasov)                                         
U|                         Yes?                                                       
C|                                     PIKALOV                                        
U|                         But the temperature is rising. And--                       
O|
N|            He trails off. Then simply hands the paper to Legasov. It's             
N|            a SPECTROGRAPH. Legasov stares at the chart for a moment.               
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         There's a spike in zirconium-95. It's                      
U|                         from the cladding on the fuel rods.                        
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Which means what?                                          
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         We use zirconium to contain the                            
U|                         uranium because its melting point is                       
U|                         so high.                                                   
O|                                     (MORE)                                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                     11.            
O|
C,E|                                     LEGASOV (cont'd)                               
U|                         A graphite fire alone isn't hot                            
U|                         enough to vaporise it. It's the fuel                       
U|                         itself.                                                    
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         The meltdown has begun.                                    
N|            The sound of a HELICOPTER buzzing by. The air churns up DUST            
N,T|            from the ground, and we DISSOLVE TO:                                    
O|
S|       315  INT. THE REACTOR - NOW                                        315       
O|
N|            AN EMPTY WATER BASIN - puddles of dark water, rust marks on             
N|            the walls, and extending down from the ceiling, a large                 
N|            DRAINAGE PIPE, like an upside-down T.                                   
O|
N,G|            We hold for a moment. Then a thin line of dark MAGMA drips              
N|            out of drain... dangles in the air, then hits the ground                
N|            with a SIZZLE.                                                          
N|            Another line from the other opening. And then, without                  
N|            warning:                                                                
O|
N|            A HUGE MASS of CORIUM POURS OUT AND DOWN - from both sides              
N|            of the drainpipe - thick, deadly lava, bubbling and POOLING             
N|            with a SIZZLE on the concrete below...                                  
O|
N|            We're watching a nuclear reactor core melting down. This is             
N|            what it looks like. Hypnotic, and almost beautiful.                     
O|
N|            Almost.                                                                 
N,T|            We hear the concrete CRACKLING, and we cut to:                          
O|
S|       316  INT. HALLWAY - RADIATION WING -HOSPITAL NO.6 - NIGHT          316       
O|
N|            Drip... drip... drip...                                                 
O|
N|            A small, rusty DRINKING FOUNTAIN drips. Each drip echoing               
N|            slightly in the:                                                        
O|
N,G|            EMPTY HOSPITAL HALLWAY - where Lyudmilla sits in a chair.               
N|            Sleeping. No sound but the low buzz of the fluorescent                  
N|            lights, and drip... drip... drip...                                     
N|            And then a low moan from off screen.                                    
O|
N|            Lyudmilla shifts a bit. Troubled sleep.                                 
O|
N|            Another moan. Another. Then a man SCREAMING in pain.                    
O|
O|
O|                                                                     12.            
O|
N|            Lyudmilla instantly wakes. It's silent in the hallway again.            
N|            Was that a dream?                                                       
O|
N|            Another SCREAM. Louder. She leaps to her feet, and:                     
O|
S|       317  INT. HOSPITAL ROOM 15 / HALLWAY - MOMENTS LATER               317       
O|
N|            Lyudmilla enters the room. Two nurses are attending to                  
N|            Vasily, blocking Lyudmilla's view of him.                               
O|
N|            The other two beds are EMPTY, save for BLOOD STAINS on both             
N|            of them.                                                                
O|
N|            The nurses are clearly struggling.                                      
O|
C|                                     HOSPITAL 6 NURSE                               
U|                         You have to stop moving. We can't get                      
U|                         the needle in if you don't stop                            
U|                         moving.                                                    
N|            His screaming is terrible. Lyudmilla moves towards the                  
N|            nurses, panicked.                                                       
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         You're hurting him!                                        
O|
N|            She pulls at one of the nurses, and sees:                               
O|
N|            VASILY - covered in LESIONS. His face, his arms, his neck...            
N|            everywhere there is skin, there are tiny open SORES, some               
N|            bleeding, some coated in weeping pus.                                   
N|            Vasily writhes in AGONY as the other nurse attempts to hold             
N|            him down by his burning skin in order to inject morphine.               
O|
N|            Lyudmilla can only stare in horror.                                     
O|
C|                                     HOSPITAL 6 NURSE                               
U|                         You can't be in here.                                      
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         What's happening to him...?                                
N|            Vasily SCREAMS again.                                                   
O|
C|                                     HOSPITAL 6 NURSE                               
U|                         YOU CAN'T BE IN HERE!                                      
O|
N|            The nurse PUSHES Lyudmilla out of the room, and then heads              
N|            back to help her colleague.                                             
O|
O|
O|                                                                     13.            
O|
N|            Lyudmilla stands alone in the hallway, helpless, listening              
N|            to her husband screaming... as if he's been set on fire...              
O|
N,G|            We hold on her listening until it's too much to bear, and:              
O|
S|       318  INT. KREMLIN CONF. ROOM / INT. COMMAND ROOM - NIGHT           318       
O|
N|            Gorbachev's grim face.                                                  
O|
N|            ON HIS DESK in the corner of the room - seven newspapers                
N|            from major Western nations. On each one, a stapled                      
N|            translation. They're all about the same thing. Chernobyl.               
O|
N|            The PHONE RINGS. He answers.                                            
O|
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
U|                         Yes.                                                       
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         Put them through.                                          
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         Well?                                                      
N,T|            INTERCUT WITH: Shcherbina on the phone in the command suite             
N|            at the Polissya. Legasov next to him, on a second handset.              
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         The graphite fire is nearly out, and                       
U|                         the bubbler tanks are being drained.                       
U|                         We have successfully eliminated the                        
U|                         risk of thermal explosion.                                 
N|            Gorbachev slowly exhales. Then realizes Shcherbina has                  
N|            fallen quiet on the other end.                                          
O|
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
U|                         And?                                                       
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         The situation inside the core is                           
U|                         deteriorating faster than                                  
U|                         anticipated. The concrete pad will                         
U|                         hold for 6 to 8 weeks, but after                           
U|                         that, Legasov estimates a 50% chance                       
U|                         the fuel will breach the pad and melt                      
U|                         down into the groundwater itself.                          
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
U|                         And where does that groundwater go?                        
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         The Pripyat River, which feeds into                        
U|                         the Dnieper.                                               
O|                                     (MORE)                                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                     14.            
O|
C,E|                                     SHCHERBINA (cont'd)                            
U|                         The primary water supply for                               
U|                         approximately fifty million people--                       
U|                         not to mention crops and livestock--                       
U|                         would be... unusable.                                      
N|            Gorbachev closes his eyes. Can't take it anymore.                       
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         We are recommending we install a heat                      
U|                         exchanger under the pad to lower the                       
U|                         core temperature and halt the                              
U|                         meltdown. In order to do this, I'm                         
U|                         told we'll need all of the liquid                          
U|                         nitrogen in the Soviet Union.                              
O|
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
P|                               (long pause, then)                                   
U|                         Alright.                                                   
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         And of course, we'll need--                                
O|
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
U|                         Whatever you need, you have it. That                       
U|                         should be clear by now. Is there                           
U|                         anything else?                                             
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         My apologies. No. Thank you for--                          
O|
N|            But:                                                                    
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Yes. I wanted to address the 30                            
U|                         kilometer exclusion zone--                                 
O|
N|            Shcherbina reacts. What does Legasov think he's doing?                  
O|
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
U|                         What exclusion zone? Is that Legasov?                      
U|                         What are you-- ?                                           
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
P|                               (jumping in quickly)                                 
U|                         Minor details, General Secretary.                          
U|                         Premier Ryzhkov has determined that--                      
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
P|                               (angry)                                              
U|                         If he determined, then he determined.                      
O|
N|            Shcherbina glares at Legasov. Idiot.                                    
O|
O|
O|                                                                     15.            
O|
C|                                     GORBACHEV                                      
U|                         Legasov, you are there for one                             
U|                         purpose, do you understand? To make                        
U|                         this stop. I don't want questions. I                       
U|                         want to know when this will be over.                       
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         If you mean, when will Chernobyl be                        
U|                         completely safe, the half-life of                          
U|                         Plutonium-239 is 24,000 years.                             
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         Perhaps we should just say, "Not                           
U|                         within our lifetimes."                                     
O|
N|            A stunned Gorbachev hangs on the phone for a few silent                 
N|            moments... and then slowly hangs up. Then:                              
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         I think you and I should take a walk.                      
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
P|                               (wary)                                               
U|                         It's late. I'm tired.                                      
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
P|                               (not negotiable)                                     
U|                         We're taking a walk.                                       
O|
S|       319  EXT. PRIPYAT - STREET - NIGHT                                 319       
N|            A DOG walks steadily, tail wagging. Happy. A bit of SAUSAGE             
N,G|            sails into frame, and the dog snatches it out of the air.               
N,G|            Now another DOG enters frame. Then a CAT.                               
O|
N|            REVEAL: Shcherbina and Legasov walking down the abandoned               
N|            city street. Street lamps shining for no one.                           
O|
N|            As they go, Shcherbina casually rips pieces of a large                  
N|            kolbasa and tosses them back to the left-behind pets, a                 
N|            dozen of whom follow him like he's some kind of Pied Piper.             
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Would you like some kolbasa?                               
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
P|                               (enough with this)                                   
U|                         What is it you want? An apology? I                         
U|                         won't sit back and let these people--                      
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         What's going to happen to our boys?                        
O|
O|
O|                                                                     16.            
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         What boys? The divers?                                     
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         The divers, the firefighters, the men                      
U|                         in the control room. What does the                         
U|                         radiation do to them? Precisely.                           
N|            Legasov doesn't want to answer this question. But:                      
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         At the levels some of them were                            
U|                         exposed to... ionizing radiation                           
U|                         tears the cellular structure apart.                        
U|                         The skin blisters, turns red, then                         
U|                         dark. There's nausea, dizziness,                           
U|                         fever, loss of consciousness.                              
N|            Shcherbina throws the last of the sausage into the night,               
N|            and the pets chase after it, disappearing into the dark.                
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Continue.                                                  
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
