U|                         The immediate effects subside. The                         
U|                         patients appear to be recovering.                          
U|                         Healthy, even. But they aren't.                            
O|
N|            They arrive at a bench. Shcherbina sits. Legasov joins him.             
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
P|                               (calmly, again)                                      
U|                         Continue.                                                  
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         This lasts for only a day or two.                          
U|                         Then the cellular damage begins to                         
U|                         manifest. The bone marrow dies, the                        
U|                         immune system fails, and the soft                          
U|                         tissue and organs begin to decompose.                      
U|                         The arteries and veins spill open                          
U|                         like sieves, to the point where you                        
U|                         can't even administer morphine for                         
U|                         the pain, which is-- unimaginable.                         
U|                         And within three days to three weeks,                      
U|                         you are dead. That is what will                            
U|                         happen to those boys.                                      
N|            Shcherbina takes it in. Then:                                           
O|
O|
O|                                                                     17.            
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Strange. How the things we can't see                       
U|                         are the most dangerous.                                    
P|                               (beat)                                               
U|                         And what about us?                                         
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         We've gotten a steady dose, but much                       
U|                         less of it. Not strong enough to kill                      
U|                         the cells, but consistent enough to                        
U|                         damage the DNA. In time... cancer. Or                      
U|                         aplastic anemia. Either way, fatal.                        
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Well. In a sense, it would seem we've                      
U|                         gotten off easily then... Valery.                          
O|
N|            His first name. Legasov notes the familiarity. The kind                 
N|            between friends. A moment between them, and then Shcherbina             
N|            just barely nods his head to the left.                                  
N|            Was that a signal? Legasov glances back down the street in              
N|            the direction of Shcherbina's nod, and sees:                            
O|
N|            THIRTY METERS BEHIND THEM - a MAN AND WOMAN under a street              
N|            lamp. Clearly following from a distance.                                
O|
N|            But not just any man and woman. The "husband and wife" from             
N|            the bar. The wife who asked Legasov if there were anything              
N|            to worry about.                                                         
N|            They don't seem worried now at all. Nor do they seem like a             
N|            husband and wife. They just stare back at Legasov.                      
O|
N|            Dead-eyed. Expressionless. And above all, no attempt to                 
N|            disguise who they truly are.                                            
O|
N|            Legasov turns back to look straight ahead. Terrified. My                
N|            god... that night... he almost told them what he knew...                
O|
N|            Shcherbina resumes walking. Legasov moves with him. Stiffly.            
N|            Trying not to look back.                                                
O|
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Now you know why I wanted to take a                        
U|                         walk. Obviously the work site is                           
U|                         bugged. But I suspect our rooms as                         
U|                         well. Even our bathrooms. They say                         
U|                         you haven't taken a proper shit until                      
U|                         you've done it in front of the KGB.                        
N|            And now Legasov understands the reason for Shcherbina's                 
N|            impassioned defense of the Soviet state.                                
O|
O|
O|                                                                     18.            
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         I've seen them before. They've been                        
U|                         here the whole time.                                       
O|
N|            Shcherbina sighs. Legasov is a smart man, and yet so stupid.            
C|                                     SHCHERBINA                                     
U|                         Of course they've been here the whole                      
U|                         time. If we're seeing them out in the                      
U|                         open now-- it's because they want us                       
U|                         to know.                                                   
O|
