N|            personal items. She hands the form across a desk to a prison            
N|            attendant, who heads into a back room with the form, leaving            
N|            them alone for a moment.                                                
O|
N|            There's something gnawing at her. Until she can't ignore it             
N|            any longer... and she turns to Legasov.                                 
O|
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         Did you know they were running a                           
U|                         safety test?                                               
O|
N|            Legasov sighs. Yes. He heard. It's madness.                             
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         There's something else. Akimov says he                     
U|                         shut the reactor down, and Toptunov                        
U|                         confirms it. They pressed AZ-5.                            
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Apparently not soon enough.                                
O|
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         No. They say Akimov pressed AZ-5, and                      
U|                         then the reactor exploded.                                 
N|            He stiffens. A jolt of fear in his stomach. She doesn't see.            
O|
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         If it had been just one of them, I                         
U|                         would have written it off as faulty                        
U|                         memory or even delusion... but they                        
U|                         both agreed. They were adamant.                            
O|
N|            She turns to him. Legasov seems lost in thought.                        
O|
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         Comrade?                                                   
N|            He snaps out of it. Turns to her.                                       
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Do you think it's possible?                                
O|
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         No. I think it makes no sense. I                           
U|                         think it's what I would say if I                           
U|                         wanted to cover my own mistakes.                           
O|
O|
O|                                                                     52.            
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         But?                                                       
O|
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         I believed them.                                           
N|            A beat, then he moves in close to her. Sotto voce, so no one            
N|            can overhear.                                                           
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         Then you should pursue it. We have to                      
U|                         pursue every possibility, no matter                        
U|                         how unlikely... and no matter what--                       
U|                         or who-- is to blame.                                      
O|
N|            Understood.                                                             
O|
N|            The prison attendant returns with a bin holding Khomyuk's               
N|            personal effects. As she gathers her items...                           
C|                                     KHOMYUK                                        
U|                         I'll go back to the hospital now and                       
U|                         reinterview Akimov and Toptunov... if                      
U|                         they're still awake.                                       
O|
C|                                     LEGASOV                                        
U|                         They're not.                                               
O|
N|            She meets his eyes. Dead? Yes. They're gone. She's surprised            
N|            by the depth of her own grief, and:                                     
N|            SOUND FADES/MUSIC RISES                                                 
O|
S,G|       347  EXT. FINAL MONTAGE                                            347       
O|
N,G|            As the music plays, we dissolve from moment to moment,                  
N|            drifting back and forth like a ghost...                                 
O|
N,G|            OUTSIDE HOSPITAL NO. 6 - Lyudmilla exits the building. Numb.            
N|            Suitcase in hand. The door closes behind her, but she                   
N|            doesn't look back. There's nothing left to see.                         
O|
N,G|            ISOLATION ROOM - Vasily's bed is now empty. An orderly                  
N|            removes the blood-stained sheets.                                       
N,G|            MOSCOW CITY STREET - Soldiers with CLIPBOARDS walk down the             
N|            street, and begin heading into apartment buildings.                     
O|
N,G|            ACROSS THE STREET - more soldiers, heading into more                    
N|            buildings. Dozens of them. Old women watch. They've seen                
N|            this before. Men will be taken now. Some won't come back.               
O|
O|
O|                                                                     53.            
O|
N,G|            HOSPITAL LOADING DOCK - SEVEN GURNEYS, each holding a                   
N|            PLASTIC-WRAPPED BODY. Two men in FULL HAZMAT SUITS lift one             
N|            of the bodies and place it into a nondescript PLYWOOD                   
N|            COFFIN.                                                                 
N,G|            MOSCOW PARKING LOT - a man at the front of a queue gets some            
N|            papers stamped and handed back to him. He moves off to the              
N,I|            left, and the next man steps up. This is PAVEL, 23, thin,               
N,I|            pale and frightened. He shivers a bit in the cold drizzle               
N,G|            that's begun to fall. He hands his documents to the unseen              
N|            officer.                                                                
O|
N|            STAMP. Papers are handed back, and Pavel is ushered off.                
O|
M|            We will see him again.                                                  
O|
N,G|            HOSPITAL LOADING DOCK - the Hazmat men roughly hammer a lid             
N|            onto the plywood coffin, then lift it and put it down into:             
N|            A LARGER, ENTIRELY METAL COFFIN. Now they lift a heavy METAL            
N|            LID, and place it on top.                                               
N,G|            HOSPITAL LOADING DOCK - a Hazmat man is WELDING the metal               
N|            coffin shut. A reflection of SHOWERING SPARKS in the clear              
N|            plastic window covering his face.                                       
O|
N,G|            As the sparks RISE to fill our view, the music FADES...                 
O|
N|            ...and is replaced by the TOLLING OF A BELL.                            
O|
S|       348  EXT. MITINSKOE CEMETERY - MOSCOW - LATE AFTERNOON             348       
N|            A small gathering of mourners stand under a gray sky. Some              
N|            are crying. Lyudmilla is not. She stares blankly ahead.                 
O|
N|            In front of the mourners, a line of soldiers.                           
O|
N,G|            We move through them to see: a large TRENCH has been dug in             
N|            the State graveyard, about thirty feet away. There are                  
N|            already SIX METAL COFFINS in the trench.                                
O|
N,G|            A TRUCK-MOUNTED CRANE moves into view, carrying the last of             
N|            the WELDED-SHUT COFFINS. It lowers it down into the trench,             
N|            where two SOLDIERS wait to hold it in place.                            
N|            The CRANE TRUCK pulls away, and the soldiers quickly                    
N|            scramble up and out of the trench. Frightened.                          
O|
N|            Lyudmilla stares straight ahead. Then, in a quiet voice, to             
N|            herself... almost a whisper...                                          
O|
O|
O|                                                                     54.            
O|
C|                                     LYUDMILLA                                      
U|                         Open wide, O earth, and receive what                       
U|                         was formed from you by the hand of God.                    
O|
N|            And now, a sound you do not hear at funerals. The deep THRUM            
N|            of a heavy motor.                                                       
N|            Lyudmilla watches, shaken, as:                                          
O|
N|            A CEMENT MIXER backs into position at the edge of the                   
N|            trench, and CONCRETE begins pouring out, FILLING THE TRENCH             
N|            with the METAL COFFINS.                                                 
O|
N|            Lyudmilla's eyes fill with tears, and she trembles from the             
N|            horror of it, but she does not look away.                               
O|
N,G|            CLOSE ON: a METAL COFFIN. The concrete rises around it, and             
N,G|            we wait and watch over the agonizing seconds...                         
N|            ...until the coffin disappears under the wet gray, and:                 
O|
T|                                                      FADE TO BLACK                 
O|
M|                                END OF EPISODE THREE                                
O|
O|
